  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nMany locations have already developed dense fog, which is expected \nto expand through the next few hours as rain affected most of the \narea at some point yesterday. The latest model runs are quite \nbullish on fog, even the HRRR, which doesn't typically show much fog \nin advance. A Dense Fog Advisory may be needed later tonight.  \n\nA shortwave trough will cross the OH Valley and central Appalachians \ntomorrow afternoon and night, followed by a surface cold front. Most \nCAMS are keeping the showers to our north, although the 00Z NAM \nshows some light QPF in our northern sections as it brings the front \nto our northern border by 12Z Monday. NBM still has no PoPs, and \nwill keep that going. \n\nThe backdoor front pushes through on Monday as a surface high moves \ninto the Mid Atlantic, but it will provide little to no relief from \nthe above normal temperatures (although dewpoints will be a little \nlower behind the front). Aloft, a ridge will be over the Southeast. \nSubsidence under this ridge will provide hot and dry conditions for \nmost of the area through the week. With forecast highs at CHA in the \nlower to mid 90s each day, the streak of days reaching 90+ degrees \nwill continue (now at 40).",
